Section 1
Basic Administration

Essential duties of a system administrator:

1. Controlling access - creating, removing and restoring
accounts for users; managing rules.
2. Adding hardware - installation and configuration of
physical hardware.
3. Automating tasks - automating repetitive and time-
consuming tasks, reducing likelyhood of a human error;
Largely requires knowledge of the scripting languages.
4. Overseeing backups - backing up and restoring data;
Requires systematic backing up and restoration to see
if it's working.
5. Handling software - selecting, installing, updating,
removing, etc., of the software; properly applying upda-
tes and patches; testing, reviewing.
6. Monitoring - benchmarking the services (example: res-
pond times; analyzing log files, etc.).
7. Troubleshooting - diagnosing arrising problems, sol-
ving them or providing them to specified experts.
8. Documentation - keeping the documentation of the lo-
cal scripts, software i.g.
9. Security - implementing security features against
malicious operators.
10. Performance tuning - keeping the OS tailored for
specified tasks.
11. Site policies - developing accessible and legal
policy relating to the site use, data retention, etc.
12. Vendor communication - communicating and implemen-
ting the third party software/hardware.
13. Firefight - helping others, solving accidental prob-
lems, etc.

Topics to know: Bash, Python, Ruby, expect (front end);

Manual pages sections:
1. User-level commands and applications
2. System calls and kernel error codes
3. Library calls
4. Device drivers and network protocols
5. Standard file formats
6. Games and demostrations
7. Misc. files and documents
8. System administration commands
9. Kernel specs and interfaces

Booting and System Management Daemons

Broadly defined boot sequence:
1. Find., load., run., boot code
2. Find., load., run., the OS kernel
3. Run., startup scripts and system daemons
4. Maintaning procesc hygiene and managing system
state transitions

Linux & UNIX boot process:
1. Power on
2. Load BIOS/UEFI from NVRAM
3. Probe for hardware
4. Select boot device
5. Identify EFI system partition
6. Load boot loader
7. Determine which kernel to boot
8. Load kernel
9. Instantiate kernel data structures
10. Start init/systemd as PID 1
11. Execute startup scripts
12. Running system

BIOS

Traditional BIOS assumes that the boot device starts
with a record called MBR - Master Boot Record.
MBR icnludes the first-stage boot loader (boot block),
and the disk partiotioning table.

Nor BIOS, nor boot loader are sophisticated enough
to read any other filesystem, so they must look
for them somewhere else.
To do this, the boot loader reads the partitioning
table from the MBR and identifies the disk partition
as active. It then reads and executes the second-stage
boot loader from the beginning of that partition.
This is called "volume boot record".

Alternatively, the SSBL can live in the dead zone
between the MBR and the first disk partition.
It contains at least 32KB of storage.

UEFI

It includes the modern disk partitioning scheme
called GPT (GUID Partition Table, GUID - globally
unique identifier). It also supports FAT -
File Allocation Table. These combined defines the
EFI System Partition - ESP.
UEFI defines API for accessing the hardware,
also provides ability to write UEFI-level add-on
device drivers.

efibootmgr - examine and modify UEFI variables.
efibootmgr -v shows the summary of a boot config.

Most bootstrapping procedures include the execution
of a boot-loader distinct from the BIOS/UEFI and
the OS kernel.
The boot-loaders main job is to identify and load an
appropriate OS kernel. It also controlls the arguments
for the kernel.

GRUB: the GRand Unified Boot loader

It comes in two versions: GRUB-legacy and
GRUB 2.

GRUB understands most filesystems, thus making
it possible to reach the root folders and read the
config file in a plain text file.

The configuration file is called -
grub.cfg. It's usually stored in /boot/grub.

Common GRUB config. options from /etc/default/grub:

1. GRUB_BACKGROUND - bkg. image
2. GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX - Kernel parameters to add to
menu entries for Linux.
3. GRUB_DEFAULT - Number or title of the default menu
entry.
4. GRUB_DISABLE_RECOVERY - Prevents the generation of
recovery mode entries.
5. GRUB_PRELOAD_MODULES - List of GRUB modules to be
loaded ASAP.
6. GRUB_TIMEOUT - Seconds to display the boot menu
before the autoboot.

After editing the /etc/default/grub file,
run either update-grub or grub2-mkconfig.

grub-mkconfig - for my machine.

GRUB supports cmdline to edit the cfg file on the fly.
To enter the cmdline mode, type c on the GRUB boot screen.

Kernel startup options usually modify the values of the
kernel parameters, instruct to probe for specific device,
specify path to init or systemd, or designate a particular
root device.

Examples:
1. debug - turns on the kernel debugging
2. init=/bin/bash - starts only the bash shell
3. root=/dev/foo - designates the /dev/foo as the
root device
4. single - boots up the single-user mode

When modified at the boot time, the options
are not persistent.

init -  a system management daemon.
Main functions of this daemon is to make sure that
the system runs with the right complement of services
and daemons.

Common defined modes:
1. Single-user mode - only a minimal set of filesystems
is mounted; no services are running; root shell is started
on the console.
2. Multiuser mode - all customary filesystems are mounted;
all configured network services are started; window system and
graphical login manager for the console are also enabled.
3. Server mode - same as multiuser mode, except no GUI for
the console.

Systemd is the main system management daemon nowadays.

It operates on entities called units.
A unit can be: a service, a socket, a device, a mount
point, etc.

Each unit is configured by the unit file.

Example:
[Unit]
Description=ZeroTier One
After=network-online.target network.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/usr/bin/zerotier-one
Restart=always
KillMode=process

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

These files usually live in /usr/lib/systemd/system.

systemctl - all-purpose command for checking and changing
the configuration.

Commonly used systemctl subcommands:
1. list-unit-files - shows installed units
2. enable - enables unit to activate at boot
3. disable - prevents unit from activating on boot
4. isolate - changes operating mode to target
5. start - starts unit immediately
6. stop - stops unit immediately
7. restart - restart the unit (or activates) immediately
8. status - shows unit's status and recent log entries
9. kill - sends a signal to unit
10. reboot - reboots the PC
11. daemon-reload - reloads the unit files in the systemd configuration

Explicit dependies in the [Unit] section in unit files:
1. Wants - units that should be coactivated if possible
2. Requires - strict dependencies, failure of any of these
terminates the service
3. Requisite - similar to Requires, but must be already
active.
4. BindsTo - similar to Requires
5. PartOf - similar to Requires, but affects only starting and
stopping
6. Conflicts - cannot be activated with these units

Access control and rootly powers

Basic rules:
1. Access control decisions depend on which user/
user group is attempting an action.
2. Objects have owners. Owners have broad, but usually
still restricted control over them.
3. You own the objects you created.
4. Root user acts as the owner of every object.
5. Only root user has access to special operations.

Groups are traditionally defined in /etc/group file.

Sudo is a preferred way to access root powers for a
single operation.

List of sudoers is stored in /etc/sudoers file.

Process control

Process - is an abstraction through which the
memory, CPU time and I/O resources can be managed.

A process consists of the address space (memory pages),
and kernel data structures.
Generally, the memory pages contain the code and libraries
which are executed in process.

The kernel data structures handle each process by recording
such data as:
1. The process address space map
2. The current status
3. The execution priority
4. The info about resources being used (CPU, RAM, etc.)
5. The info about opened files and network ports
6. The process signal mask (what signals are blocked)
7. The owner of a process

PID - process ID number
The kernal assigns unique ID number to every process.
They are assigned in order as processes are created.
Programs and calls may require the exact PID.

PPID - parent PID
UNIX nor Linux in themselves have system calls that
initiate a new process. Instead, and existing program
should create a clone of itself, which then changes
the program it runs.

The process from which cloning has been done is
referred as a Parent Process, and PPID refers to the
PID of that process.

UID & EUID - real and effective user ID
UID is a copy of the parent process UID, is the user
ID of a person who created it.

EUID is an effective UID, it determines what resources
and files the process can access.

Linux also includes FSUID, a filesystem access
permissions.

GID & EGID - real and effective group ID
GID stands for group ID of a process. The relation
between GID and EGID is the same as for UID and EUID.

Niceness
Is a process scheduling priority. It determines how
much of the CPU time the process receives.

The lifecycle of a process
Process copies itself using the system call named fork.
The copy is largely identical to the parent process.

After the fork, a child process usually uses the exec routine.

When a process compeletes, it calls a routine named _exit,
notifying the kernel that it's ready to die and provides
an exit code.

The kernel also provides the exit status code to the parent
process of a said completed process. If a parent process
dies for some reason before the child is completed,
the kernel sets the parent of said process to be itself.

Most important signals table:
1. HUP - Hangup - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
2. INT - Interrupt - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
3. QUIT - Quit - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: Y
9. KILL - Kill - Terminate - C: N, B: N, D: N
10. BUS - Bus error - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: Y
11. SEGV - Segmentation fault - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: Y
15. TERM - Software Termination - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
17. STOP - Stop - Stop - C: N, B: N, D: N
18. TSTP - Keyboard stop - Stop - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
19. CONT - Continue after stop - Ignore - C: Y, B: N, D: N
28. WINCH - Window changed - Ignore - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
30. USR1 - User defined #1 - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: N
30. USR2 - User defined #2 - Terminate - C: Y, B: Y, D: N

kill - used to send any signal, defaults to TERM.

kill [-signal] pid

killall - kills processes by the name.

sudo killall httpd

pkill - searches for a process by name (or, for example,
EUID) and sends a specified signal.

sudo pkill -u ben

Sends TERM to all processes running under user ben.

Process can be sleeping, when all of its threads are sleeping.
This means it doesn't receive any CPU time until it gets
an input.

Sometimes a process may finish the execution, but it's parent
process doesn't collect the status info in time. This is a
so-called "zombie" process.
In ps, they usually appear as <exiting> or <defunct>.

ps aux - show all running processes.

ps lax - same, but the UIDs are not translated to names.

Common ps aux output:
1. USER - Username of the process's owner
2. PID - Process ID
3. %CPU - Percentage of the CPU used
4. %MEM - Percentage of the real memory used
5. VSZ - Virtual size of a process
6. RSS - Resident set size (number of pages in memory)
7. TTY - Control terminal ID
8. STAT - Current process status: R - Runnable;
S - Sleeping; Z - Zombie; D - in an uninterruptible sleep;
T - Traced or stopped
9. TIME - CPU time consumed
10. COMMAND - Command name and arguments

Grep needed process while hiding the grep
from the list:
ps aux | grep -v grep | grep systemd

Determine PID with full path:
pidof /usr/lib/systemd/systemd

Determine PID with the process's name:
pgrep systemd

/proc - is a pseudo filesystem that provides interface
for the kernel data structures.

/proc files and their contents:
1. cgroup - The control groups to which the process belongs
2. cmd - Command or the program the process executes
3. cmdline - Complete command line of the process (\0 sep.)
4. cwd - Symlink to the process's current directory
5. environ - The process's environment variables (\0 sep.)
6. exe - Symlink to the file being executed
7. fd - subdir. containing links for each open file descriptor
8. fdinfo - subdir. containing further info for each open file desc.
9. maps - Memory mapping information (shared segments, libraries, etc.)
10. ns - Subdir. with symlinks for each namespace used by the process
11. root - Symlink to the process's root directory (set with chroot)
12. stat - General process status info
13. statm - Memory usage info

strace [-argument] PID - display every system call of the process.

Runaway process - is a process that uses too much of the CPU time,
memory, network, etc., than expected.

Systemd timers
Systemd timer usually consists of 2 files:
1. Timer unit - describes the schedule
2. Service unit - specifies what has to be executed

Systemd timer types:
1. OnActiveSec - Relative to the timer activation itself
2. OnBootSec - Relative to the system boot
3. OnStartupSec - Relative to systemd activation
4. OnUnitActiveSec - Relative to the time a specified
unit was last active
5. OnUnitInactive - Relative to the time a specified
unit was last inactive
6. OnCalendar - A specific day and time

systemctl list-timers - lists all active timers, --all
lists even inactive.

Filesystem

fuser -v - list all processes attached to a directory.

/boot - usually contains all kernel related files.
/etc - critical system and configuration files.
/sbin & /bin - important utilities
/tmp - temporary files
/usr - stadard, non-critical programs.
/var - spool directories, log files, accounting info, etc.

man hier - filesystem hierarchy rationale.

Main file types:
1. Regular file
2. Directory
3. Character device file
4. Block device file
5. Local domain sockets
6. Named pipes (FIFO)
7. Symbolic link

file - command to check the file type.

man ascii - info about ascii chars.

chmod - change mode (access).

Permission encoding for chmod:

OCTAL	BINARY	PERMS
0	000	---
1	001	--x
2	010	-w-
3	011	-wx
4	100	r--
5	101	r-x
6	110	rw-
7	111	rwx

Software installation and management

The most appropriate way to install the OS
on the multiple system (>10) at once is using network
protocols.
Common methods include DHCP, TFTP to boot the the system
sans (Storage area network) physical media. They then
retrieve the OS installation files from a network server
using HTTP, NFS or FTP.

PXE - Preboot eXecution Environment - lets system
boot from the network interface. It acts as a mini-
ature OS that sits in the ROM on the network card.

PXE boot and installation process algorithm:
1. CL - DHCP request (Includes PXE options)
2. SV - DHCP response (points to the TFTP boot server)
3. CL - Request boot image via TFTP
4. SV - Serve boot image and config
5. CL - Request installation image via HTTP/NFS/other
6. SV - Serve installation files

Common PXE boot systems:
1. PXELINUX
2. iPXE

Scripting and the shell

Main principles:
1. Write small scripts
Write small scripts that do one job at a time.
For example, a simple backup function
for bash shell:
	function backup () {
			newname= 1$.'date +%Y-%m-%d.%H%M.bak';
			mv $1 $newname;
			echo "Backed up $1 to $newname";
			cp -p $newname $1;
		}

2. Learn just few tools thoroughly
3. Automate repetitive work
4. Do not optimize prematurely
5. Pick the scripting language
6. Stick to the practices

User management
